WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Christopher Riley, Screenwriter, Author, and&nbsp;
Instructor: Individually we can have defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:08.400
moments, families can have defining moments&nbsp;
and certainly characters if they are lifelike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.960
we'll have moments that define them as well.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.040
Film Courage:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.200
Can you tell us why the movie Ordinary&nbsp;
People was a defining moment in your life?

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:23.840
Christopher: I think when I saw Ordinary People&nbsp;
I was about 17 years old and it was the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.840
really serious movie I had seen&nbsp;
after Chitty Chitty Bang Bang and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.120
it also really spoke to who I was as a kid. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:48.400
kind of a socially awkward lonely kid and so I&nbsp;
felt whatever discomfort went with with that and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:57.040
Ordinary People showed this main character who&nbsp;
looked on the outside like he had everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:06.640
together and like he didn't experience the kind&nbsp;
of pain that I experienced but then the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:15.600
because it's a beautifully told story reveals his&nbsp;
secret life and in his secret life he's actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:24.160
just come out of treatment after a suicide attempt&nbsp;
and he's going to high school, he's singing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:31.600
in the choir, he looks to everyone like he's&nbsp;
one of the ones who's really got it all together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:39.600
and for the first time as I sat in the audience&nbsp;
I realized oh it's possible that what's on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:47.280
surface isn't what's really going on underneath&nbsp;
that's I guess something adults know I didn't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:55.040
that as a 17 year old and so that film showed&nbsp;
me that I wasn't the only one so that was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.240
was the first thing that made a huge difference&nbsp;
to me and then the second thing was I realized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.120
oh so the people around me might actually&nbsp;
be in pain even though it's not obvious I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:13.360
need to treat people differently and that&nbsp;
is something that has continued to shape me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.800
decades later I look at people differently&nbsp;
because of what that film taught me about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:28.320
other human beings and then the third thing for me&nbsp;
was I saw for the first time what a movie could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:34.800
and so I thought this is what I want to&nbsp;
do I want to tell these kinds of stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:45.520
that reveal characters and that move people&nbsp;
that provide meaningful emotion so for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:53.600
seeing Ordinary People became a dividing line&nbsp;
in my own life on multiple levels and so for me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.520
that is a defining moment and I assume you saw&nbsp;
it in the theater I did I saw it in the theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:05.440
I owned the dvd it's still wrapped in&nbsp;
cellophane because I can't bear to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:11.520
back and watch it I'm afraid it either is&nbsp;
not as good as I remember or it will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:20.560
I don't know I don't want it to disappoint me&nbsp;
and so I just keep it under wraps yeah yeah I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:24.800
agree with doing something it's almost like it's&nbsp;
frozen in time there's something special there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:32.160
that's exactly right yeah and I don't want to&nbsp;
disturb that memory because that memory is vivid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:40.000
and is kind of a bedrock of the person I've become&nbsp;
and how were other people leaving the theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:47.760
after watching that film I think other people were&nbsp;
powerfully moved by it Mary Tyler Moore delivers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:58.480
fantastic performance as the sort of cold-hearted&nbsp;
mother who is the whole time she's on screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:04.880
you fear she's driving her son to suicide and I&nbsp;
remember wanting to like take my shoe off in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.880
theater and show it at this throw it at the screen&nbsp;
when she was on screen which is what a testament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:18.240
to to that performance so I think other people&nbsp;
were really moved I'm not sure how many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:25.360
would point back to that experience as a defining&nbsp;
moment in their lives it's one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:33.600
about film I think that is fascinating to&nbsp;
me that we all have different responses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:41.360
to it that are partly shaped by the film itself&nbsp;
but partly shaped by us and what we bring to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:48.320
film who are the characters and Ordinary People&nbsp;
so you've got the dad played by Donald Sutherland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:56.160
the mom played by Mary Tyler Moore and then the&nbsp;
17 year old son and then there's a there's another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:05.200
brother who's really absent from the movie because&nbsp;
he drowned before the events of the movie that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:12.640
a defining moment for that family and each one&nbsp;
of these characters responds in a different way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:21.920
Mary Tyler Moore's mother character kind of makes&nbsp;
it clear to her surviving son that she wishes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:32.960
the other brother had survived and that becomes&nbsp;
the seed of his misery is that sense of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:42.160
rejected by his mother and his dad is trying to&nbsp;
figure out how to put his family back together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:50.320
after the tragic loss of the one son and the just&nbsp;
the terrible chemistry that's going on between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:59.520
mother and the surviving son played by Timothy&nbsp;
Hutton and they're all doing the best they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:10.240
but it feels so real at least to me sitting in&nbsp;
the theater it felt like this is a real family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:16.400
I didn't feel like I was watching a movie&nbsp;
and and I think that that's also informed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:25.600
even the way that I approach writing as a&nbsp;
screenwriter that's the tone I go for I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:34.960
naturalism I don't write big bombastic comic book&nbsp;
kinds of material because that's not what speaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:45.920
to me it is that sense of I've gotten lost in&nbsp;
a dream and this feels in some ways more real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:56.960
than reality because a great film reveals more of&nbsp;
the characters than sometimes I'm able to discover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.840
of the people that I actually&nbsp;
know and I find I really crave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:10.960
understanding who's really there and so a film&nbsp;
that shows that to me I really love and I strive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:17.760
to do that as a storyteller as well to really peel&nbsp;
back the layers and reveal who the characters are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:26.800
that we're dealing with I'm just curious when did&nbsp;
you purchase the dvd to Ordinary People I probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:33.360
bought that something like 10 years ago I think&nbsp;
I just came across it on a shelf somewhere and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:41.280
when I first had that thing in my hands I&nbsp;
thought well I should I should watch this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:47.120
and that I just kind of felt resistance in&nbsp;
myself to that and so then it ended up on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:53.120
my shelf of DVDs and now it's even hard to find&nbsp;
a dvd player in the house if I wanted to watch it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:01.200
can you give us a general explanation of what a&nbsp;
defining moment is Kathy who's my co-author and my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:11.840
wife and I first encountered the idea of defining&nbsp;
moments through a mentor of ours a television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:18.320
writer producer named Coleman Locke and he was&nbsp;
mentoring us through a rewrite of a feature film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:29.360
that we we had written early in our careers called&nbsp;
after the truth and Coleman made us work really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:36.240
really hard he told us we would come to hate&nbsp;
him and I thought that that was hyperbole but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:42.400
he was right he pushed us so hard we didn't come&nbsp;
to hate him we have a tremendous respect for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:48.160
but I remember foolishly asking him at one&nbsp;
point do other writers really work this hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:56.800
and I've come to find out yes they do the good&nbsp;
ones do but he he had this idea that he shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:06.320
with us and the idea was all of us have a handful&nbsp;
of experiences that have profoundly shaped us so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.520
we live through lots of moments every day&nbsp;
in our lives but not every moment is equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:19.040
in terms of its impact on shaping us and Coleman&nbsp;
believed that there were about maybe a half dozen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:29.040
moments that each one of us could point to that&nbsp;
account for much of the person that we are today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:35.520
and his idea was that the same would be true of&nbsp;
characters and if we wanted to deeply understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:44.480
our characters what drives them how that broken&nbsp;
character got broken or how that broken character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:52.800
eventually heals that we would locate that&nbsp;
half dozen or so moments that have defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:57.760
those characters and that that would actually&nbsp;
be more valuable than understanding how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:05.520
change is in the character's pocket or so many of&nbsp;
the infinite number of details that we could know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:14.240
about about a character Coleman's thought was it&nbsp;
is these moments that define the character that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.200
tell you more than anything&nbsp;
else you could possibly know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:27.280
and so Kathy and I began doing that work with&nbsp;
our characters looking into their pasts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:33.760
discover the moments that it shaped them and&nbsp;
then looking into the time of the story the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:40.320
time that's actually unfolding in a movie or&nbsp;
television episode and saying what are the the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:48.800
moments that happened live in front of our eyes&nbsp;
that not only have formed a character but now in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.640
in real time before us transform the&nbsp;
character into who they're becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:03.920
so for us what we discovered about&nbsp;
defining moments that helps us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:11.040
locate them is they always tend to create&nbsp;
this boundary between before and after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:18.240
we talk about the time before the house burned&nbsp;
down and the time after the house burned down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:23.520
all of us on this planet will talk&nbsp;
about the time before the pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:33.440
and please god the time after the pandemic at some&nbsp;
point so the planet can have a defining moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:40.960
individually we can have defining moments families&nbsp;
can have defining moments and certainly characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:48.400
if they are lifelike we'll have moments that&nbsp;
define them as well how many defining moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:54.400
are there in Ordinary People boy in Ordinary&nbsp;
People because it's been so many decades since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.400 --> 00:12:02.160
I saw the film that I can just say off the top of&nbsp;
my head there was the moment of the drowning so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:10.400
when the oldest brother dies tragically that&nbsp;
is a moment that defines the whole family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:18.880
and then also and so that happens before the&nbsp;
movie begins and then another defining event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:29.440
is when Timothy Hutton's character attempt suicide&nbsp;
and that creates another before and after moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:37.840
for purposes of the film the impact&nbsp;
of that attempted suicide is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:45.520
we as the audience as soon as we know about that&nbsp;
are on the edge of our seats because we fear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:54.160
he's going to complete the act that he's&nbsp;
going to take his life and we like this kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.720 --> 00:13:00.080
and we view that that would be tragic and&nbsp;
so we are rooting for that not to happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:08.880
we are infuriated by his mother's coldness to&nbsp;
him every time he tries to connect with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:18.160
she pushes him away and we just want to scream&nbsp;
at her don't you see what's happening don't you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:26.000
see how much he needs you can't you put your&nbsp;
arms around your son all of that is rooted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:33.360
that defining moment of the suicide attempt which&nbsp;
is made known to the audience so that we we get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:41.520
the good out of it we get the emotional juice that&nbsp;
flows from that and then there's a defining moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:49.200
of a kind of connection if I am remembering the&nbsp;
movie correctly that happens between Timothy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:56.880
Hutton and his dad Donald Sutherland that&nbsp;
makes you believe that maybe they're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.880 --> 00:14:05.520
to make it and so it creates this other turning&nbsp;
point a kind of a kind of provisional healing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:11.840
there may be more in there those are&nbsp;
the three that that remain in my memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:20.320
so it's a film of a lot of pain and&nbsp;
grief but it's also a hopeful film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:31.040
but it's bittersweet because the mom's character&nbsp;
doesn't get to participate in that reconciliation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:36.080
and again if I go back and never crack open&nbsp;
that dvd I may find out I'm remembering wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:43.840
but in my memory those are the three key defining&nbsp;
moments that immediately come to my mind.

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:46.567
What movie/TV show was a&nbsp;
defining moment in your life?

